WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.220
So after having computed the scene graph, the next thing to do in large scale 3D reconstruction

00:06.220 --> 00:13.240
would be the structure for motion to get the sparse 3D reconstruction.

00:13.240 --> 00:16.680
In general, there are three paradigms of structure for motion.

00:16.680 --> 00:22.000
The incremental structure for motion, global structure for motion, as well as the hierarchical

00:22.000 --> 00:23.000
structure for motion.

00:23.000 --> 00:28.360
We will look at incremental structure for motion in more detail and I will just briefly

00:28.360 --> 00:32.840
go through global and hierarchical structure for motion in this lecture.

00:32.840 --> 00:39.040
As the name implies, it does structure for motion in an incremental way.

00:39.040 --> 00:47.080
First we will choose from the scene graph any two views where the translation is a non-zero

00:47.080 --> 00:48.080
translation.

00:48.080 --> 00:54.120
This means that we omit any two views with pure rotation and this can be computed or

00:54.120 --> 01:00.240
obtained from the two view geometry that we use to compute the scene graph.

01:00.240 --> 01:06.080
And there are many pairs of images in the scene graph that might fulfil this criteria

01:06.080 --> 01:08.400
over here, the first criteria over here.

01:08.400 --> 01:14.560
And the best choice out of all these possible choices would be the one that has the highest

01:14.560 --> 01:15.920
inlier count.

01:15.920 --> 01:22.240
And the reason is because we learned in the earlier lecture that the computation of our

01:22.240 --> 01:29.520
fundamental matrix and all the essential matrices is essentially a least square computation.

01:29.520 --> 01:34.840
And a least square computation would be much more accurate when we have more observations

01:34.840 --> 01:36.400
from the correspondences.

01:36.400 --> 01:44.400
Hence, we want to choose the initial pair, which we also call the seed pair of images

01:44.400 --> 01:51.480
to do our incremental structure for motion as the one with the highest inlier count.

01:51.480 --> 01:56.080
So after we have chosen this seed pair, the next thing to do would be to use the eight-point

01:56.080 --> 02:01.360
algorithm to compute the fundamental matrix or the essential matrix for non-planar scene.

02:01.360 --> 02:08.000
Alternatively, we can also use the four-point algorithm to compute the homography for planar

02:08.000 --> 02:09.000
scene.

02:09.000 --> 02:13.860
And in both cases, we can use the fundamental matrix, essential matrix or the homography

02:13.860 --> 02:22.180
to decompose it into the camera matrices for this first two views.

02:22.180 --> 02:26.140
We will also set the scale to one.

02:26.140 --> 02:31.460
What this means is that after computing the fundamental matrix, or especially the essential

02:31.460 --> 02:37.260
matrix, which you can decompose to the rotation and translation, we will simply set the scale

02:37.260 --> 02:39.220
of the translation to one.

02:39.220 --> 02:45.100
And in the case of the fundamental matrix, we recall that from the lecture of fundamental

02:45.100 --> 02:52.860
matrix, the fundamental matrix can be decomposed into two camera projection matrices P and

02:52.860 --> 03:00.300
P', where we will simply set this P' to a scale of one.

03:00.300 --> 03:07.660
And the next step after we have obtained this P and P', the two camera projection matrices

03:07.660 --> 03:15.900
of the seat views, then we will do a triangulation based on the inlier correspondences from RANSAC,

03:15.900 --> 03:22.020
from the robust estimation of the fundamental matrix, essential matrix or the homography.

03:22.020 --> 03:26.620
And we will do this using the linear triangulation algorithm that we have seen earlier on to

03:26.620 --> 03:28.380
get the 3D points.

03:28.380 --> 03:36.460
So for these two views, for example, X1, J in the first view, and we'll use I and J to

03:36.460 --> 03:40.820
denote the view index as well as the point index.

03:40.820 --> 03:46.460
And in the second view, it will be the correspondence of X, I, J would be X2, J. And we will make

03:46.460 --> 03:52.260
use of these image correspondences as well as the two camera projection matrices to do

03:52.260 --> 03:56.540
a linear triangulation to get the 3D point X, J.

03:56.540 --> 04:01.620
And once we have done this, we will apply the bundle adjustment, we will talk about

04:01.620 --> 04:07.540
the detail later, to refine the camera matrices as well as the 3D points by minimizing the

04:07.540 --> 04:09.780
reprojection errors.

04:09.780 --> 04:15.420
And subsequently, after we have these first two views and the reconstructed 3D points,

04:15.420 --> 04:20.580
we will subsequently add more views into the reconstruction.

04:20.580 --> 04:27.820
And this is also why this method or this paradigm of structure for motion is called incremental

04:27.820 --> 04:34.220
structure for motion, because we start off from a two view, seed views over here, and

04:34.220 --> 04:39.580
then we incrementally add on new views into the reconstruction.

04:39.580 --> 04:44.500
So an example here would be, we start off with P1, P2, and then we reconstruct all these

04:44.500 --> 04:53.820
3D points with the seed views of P1, P2, and we'll do image correspondences for P3, which

04:53.820 --> 04:56.020
is linked to P2 in the scene graph.

04:56.020 --> 05:02.380
So for example, let's say if I choose this as my seed view, and this is my P1, and this

05:02.380 --> 05:10.100
is my P2, so I would choose from P2 over here all the other possible paths from those that

05:10.100 --> 05:12.060
link to P2, for example.

05:12.060 --> 05:17.700
So suppose that I chose this view over here, because it's linked to P2, and this I would

05:17.700 --> 05:22.420
call P3 to add on to the structure for motion pipeline.

05:22.420 --> 05:30.060
And now P3, once I decided P3 from the third view from the scene graph, the next thing

05:30.060 --> 05:34.340
that I would do would be to establish the 2D-2D correspondences.

05:34.340 --> 05:42.860
So 2D-2D correspondences, and this is done by the image correspondences, so I can extract

05:42.860 --> 05:48.860
the seed feature from here, for example, and then I will establish these correspondences

05:48.860 --> 05:51.300
from P3 to P2.

05:51.300 --> 05:58.060
So once I have established the 2D-2D correspondences, I can see that from this correspondence, for

05:58.060 --> 06:03.660
example, I know that these two points are correspondences from the 2D-2D correspondences,

06:03.660 --> 06:10.460
and I know that the corresponding point in the second view is used to reconstruct this

06:10.460 --> 06:13.700
particular 3D point over here.

06:13.700 --> 06:23.780
So from this relation, I'll be able to establish the 2D-3D correspondences over here.

06:23.780 --> 06:32.380
And by doing this for all the other 2D image features on P3 to P2, I'll be able to establish

06:32.380 --> 06:40.880
a set of 2D-3D correspondences from P3 to the existing 3D point in the scene.

06:40.880 --> 06:49.800
So once we have established the 2D-3D correspondences, we can use the PMP algorithm to compute P3,

06:49.800 --> 06:55.800
the pose of the third image, with respect to the 3D scene, and this would be with respect

06:55.800 --> 07:03.640
to the world frame as well, since we are expressing all the 3D coordinates of the points with

07:03.640 --> 07:07.120
respect to a 3D world frame.

07:07.120 --> 07:11.920
And once we have done this, the next thing that we can do would be to establish more

07:11.920 --> 07:20.080
correspondences, other correspondences, between P2 and P3, such that we can do linear triangulation

07:20.080 --> 07:23.680
on these four correspondences between P2 and P3.

07:23.680 --> 07:30.240
Since P3 now is already computed by the PMP algorithm, we can use it to do a linear triangulation

07:30.240 --> 07:33.280
of additional points and add it to the scene.

07:33.280 --> 07:38.640
And we can do this for subsequent views, for example, a fourth view, where we establish

07:38.640 --> 07:44.320
the 2D-2D correspondences first, and use it to establish the 2D-3D correspondences to

07:44.320 --> 07:48.320
compute P4 using the PMP algorithm.

07:48.320 --> 07:55.080
Similarly, we can find additional correspondences between P3 and P4, and do linear triangulation

07:55.080 --> 07:56.560
to get more points.

07:56.560 --> 08:06.280
And we do this for many other views until we complete every image in the scene graph.

08:06.280 --> 08:14.720
Now once we get the initial reconstruction from the two views, fundamental matrix, essential

08:14.720 --> 08:20.600
matrix or homography, as well as PMP to add in more views in the incremental structure

08:20.600 --> 08:21.600
for motion.

08:21.600 --> 08:30.360
Finally, we can do a refinement, a non-linear refinement, in the multi-view setting, over

08:30.360 --> 08:34.960
the multi-view camera poses as well as the 3D structures, with what we call the banded

08:34.960 --> 08:35.960
adjustment.

08:35.960 --> 08:40.880
So essentially what this means is that we are going to minimize the reprojection error.

08:40.880 --> 08:49.040
So for every single camera pose, we are going to project the 3D points that is seen in that

08:49.040 --> 08:54.920
particular image and minimize its error with the corresponding observation of the point

08:54.920 --> 09:00.800
in that particular view over the camera matrices as well as the 3D points.

09:00.800 --> 09:04.820
We'll talk about this in more detail in the next few slides.

09:04.820 --> 09:10.280
The second paradigm of structure for motion would be the global structure for motion.

09:10.280 --> 09:17.200
And what this means is that the first step here, we will still make use of the scene

09:17.200 --> 09:20.840
graph, but the question can be posed in this way.

09:20.840 --> 09:26.960
For every edge in the scene graph, we will compute the relative pose, which is represented

09:26.960 --> 09:34.560
by R and T. So these are all the relative poses between every pair of images that is

09:34.560 --> 09:38.280
linked by the scene graph edges.

09:38.280 --> 09:44.520
And here we can easily compute these relative poses from either the fundamental matrix or

09:44.520 --> 09:50.640
the essential matrix or the homography and depending on whether this is a tool view.

09:50.640 --> 09:56.440
So in the usual case, we wouldn't use homography because most of the scenes in the 3D worlds

09:56.440 --> 09:57.860
are non-planar.

09:57.860 --> 10:01.680
So the homography can only be applied to planar scenes.

10:01.680 --> 10:05.960
And a preferred choice or much more accurate choice is actually the essential matrix when

10:05.960 --> 10:12.860
the camera is calibrated because we wouldn't have a projective ambiguity here.

10:12.860 --> 10:18.980
So once we have computed all the relative transformation, which we can decompose from

10:18.980 --> 10:23.140
the essential matrix, so the essential matrix will give us R and T.

10:23.140 --> 10:29.620
So for every one of this pair of images in the scene graph, we can compute the relative

10:29.620 --> 10:35.640
transformation from the essential matrix and we do obtain this particular graph over here.

10:35.640 --> 10:42.060
So where each one of the nodes here represents a camera location.

10:42.060 --> 10:47.820
So there is an absolute location at each one of the points here.

10:47.820 --> 10:53.100
And now what we are given here, the edges, the relative pose, the R and T between each

10:53.100 --> 10:54.520
one of the nodes here.

10:54.520 --> 11:01.020
So this, let's say, if I were to denote this as R1 and T1, for example, this would be the

11:01.020 --> 11:08.780
global pose of that particular camera with respect to a global frame, fw, for example.

11:08.780 --> 11:16.180
And this would be R2, T2, for example, and the relative edge over here would be denoted

11:16.180 --> 11:23.300
as R12 and T12 between the two views of 1 and 2.

11:23.300 --> 11:30.940
So similarly, we can denote this over here as R3, T3, the absolute pose with respect

11:30.940 --> 11:33.540
to the world frame.

11:33.540 --> 11:40.260
And the relative pose between these two views would be denoted as R23 and T23.

11:40.260 --> 11:47.340
So we will do this for every single node in the graph to represent it as an absolute pose.

11:47.340 --> 11:53.980
Problem now becomes, given all the relative measurements, all the relative rotation and

11:53.980 --> 12:01.140
translation between every single pair of views in our scene graph, can we find a way to compute

12:01.140 --> 12:08.580
the absolute poses of all these camera poses in the scene graph?

12:08.580 --> 12:13.260
And the way to do this can be simply summarized as follows.

12:13.260 --> 12:19.380
We'll first estimate the global rotation by minimizing this cost function over here.

12:19.380 --> 12:26.580
So what we are trying to do here is that Ri and Rj here represents the global pose of

12:26.580 --> 12:30.880
any pair of images, for example, R1 and R2.

12:30.880 --> 12:36.040
So Ri is this guy over here, and Rj is this guy over here.

12:36.040 --> 12:42.840
And what we want to do here is that we want to minimize by computing the relative transformation

12:42.840 --> 12:49.560
which is given by Ri and Rj transpose over here, we'll be able to compute the relative

12:49.560 --> 12:54.340
rotation between these two poses that is given by R12.

12:54.340 --> 12:59.240
And since R12 is what we observe over here, and these are the unknown global poses that

12:59.240 --> 13:01.620
we have to find out.

13:01.620 --> 13:06.080
In other words, what we want to do would be to minimize, to find out what are all the

13:06.080 --> 13:11.760
global poses such that when we formulate it in this way, when we compute the relative

13:11.760 --> 13:18.920
transformation between these two rotations over here, we'll be able to minimize its difference

13:18.920 --> 13:27.480
from what we observe, the relative transformation of the rotation between Ri and Rj.

13:27.480 --> 13:34.040
And in order to solve for this optimization, the first step that we need to do would be

13:34.040 --> 13:41.200
to convert the rotation matrix, which is a 3x3 matrix in the special octagonal group.

13:41.200 --> 13:50.920
In short, we denote this as SO3 group to omega, which is a vector of three dimensions, such

13:50.920 --> 13:57.980
that the 3x3 rotation matrix is given by this formulation over here, exponential of

13:57.980 --> 14:04.040
the skew symmetric matrix, so this is a 3x3 skew symmetric matrix that is obtained from

14:04.040 --> 14:11.700
the omega over here, which is in the R3 space, and we call this the exponential map.

14:11.700 --> 14:18.420
That means that the mapping function that takes in omega and outputs R, and the inverse

14:18.420 --> 14:24.940
of this exponential map, would be denoted as the logarithmic map that is given by the

14:24.940 --> 14:30.640
skew symmetric matrix over here, 3x3 matrix that is formed by the omega over here, would

14:30.640 --> 14:39.620
be equal to the log of R. And so, more specifically, what we wish to do here is that given this

14:39.620 --> 14:45.900
rotation over here, which is a 3x3 matrix, we want to reformulize this, or reformulate

14:45.900 --> 14:54.780
this cost function over here into the format of omega, where omega is just a vector of

14:54.780 --> 15:02.220
three, so that we can actually do an optimization over the three parameters over here.

15:02.220 --> 15:09.620
So in other words, what we can do over here would be to simply compute the logarithmic

15:09.620 --> 15:18.180
map. Given Rij, for example, we want to compute the logarithmic map and convert it into omega,

15:18.180 --> 15:24.580
and similarly, for the unknowns Ri and Rj's and all the global rotations over here, we

15:24.580 --> 15:31.720
can put it into the logarithmic map to get omega. And more specifically, given a rotation

15:31.720 --> 15:38.420
matrix over here that looks like this, which is a 3x3 matrix, omega can be obtained from

15:38.420 --> 15:46.220
this equation over here. So after we have converted the rotation matrix into omega using

15:46.220 --> 15:54.820
the logarithmic map, we can rewrite this relation over here, Rij equals to Rij transpose into

15:54.820 --> 16:02.420
the linear equation in terms of omega over here, where omega ij is the equivalent of

16:02.420 --> 16:10.300
Rij after we put in the logarithmic map, and we get omega ij, which can also be written

16:10.300 --> 16:20.800
as omega j minus omega i, and that is representing the relative transformation between the two

16:20.800 --> 16:26.620
global rotations over here. And what we can do here is that we can rewrite this particular

16:26.620 --> 16:35.300
omega j and omega i into this linear form over here, where omega global is simply a

16:35.300 --> 16:45.080
vector of omega 1, omega 2, and all the way until omega n, where n here denotes the number

16:45.080 --> 16:55.620
of global poses in the scene graph. And aij would be simply made out of all the identity

16:55.620 --> 17:04.100
matrices, where it would be identity if it corresponds to that selection of that particular

17:04.100 --> 17:14.660
pose in that location, otherwise it would be zero. And then now this particular minimization

17:14.660 --> 17:21.740
function over here of Rij can be solved as a least square problem, because we have simply

17:21.740 --> 17:29.620
reformulated it into this linear homogeneous system of equations, and it could be rewritten

17:29.620 --> 17:37.760
as A omega global equals to omega relative over here, this is omega relative, where A

17:37.760 --> 17:44.900
and omega relative are simply made out of stacking multiple of these equations together,

17:44.900 --> 17:53.660
all the relative poses together. So every Rij over here will give us one equation over

17:53.660 --> 17:59.960
here, and we can do this for every single edge that we have in our scene graph. And

17:59.960 --> 18:05.440
by stacking all these equations together, we will get A omega global equals to omega

18:05.440 --> 18:13.560
relative. Now the unknowns over here would be omega global, which we can solve for it

18:13.560 --> 18:21.820
using the linearly square algorithm. And once we have computed omega global, we can convert

18:21.820 --> 18:28.040
it back into the rotation matrix by using the inverse of the logarithmic map, which

18:28.040 --> 18:33.780
is the exponential map as shown in this equation over here. So similarly, we can solve for

18:33.780 --> 18:39.940
the global translations using similar methods. But one complication over here is that the

18:39.940 --> 18:47.040
scale between every relative translation is unknown. So we can formulate it as Tij,

18:47.040 --> 18:51.920
which is the relative translation between any two views, let's call this Ti and this

18:51.920 --> 18:58.460
Tj over here. So the relative translation over here would be Tij, and this is all computed

18:58.460 --> 19:06.180
up to a scale. And we will normalize that scale to one by simply, after we compute T

19:06.180 --> 19:10.860
from the essential matrix, for example, we will simply normalize this observation to

19:10.860 --> 19:17.840
be equal to one. And now we want to make sure that the difference between the observed Tij

19:17.840 --> 19:23.580
over here, it's minimized with respect to the relative transformation that is computed

19:23.580 --> 19:32.080
from global post Ti and Tj over here. So we can formalize this as Ti minus Tj, and taking

19:32.080 --> 19:39.720
the difference between this and Tij that is observed from the computation of the essential

19:39.720 --> 19:48.760
matrix. And of course, we have to also normalize this particular Tij over here. And we'll follow

19:48.760 --> 19:54.480
the same method as before, as what we have seen earlier on in our rotation matrix, which

19:54.480 --> 20:00.800
we can cast this into a least square problem of Ax equals to b, and we will solve for x,

20:00.840 --> 20:11.200
which consists of all the unknown global translations. Then after we have gotten all the global poses

20:11.200 --> 20:19.420
from the in the scene graph, the next thing that we can do would be to compute the 3D

20:19.420 --> 20:25.080
scene points using the linear triangulation algorithm with the image correspondences as

20:25.080 --> 20:30.520
well as all the global poses that we have obtained from our global structure for motion

20:30.520 --> 20:36.400
technique that was described earlier on. And once we have obtained all the camera poses as well as

20:36.400 --> 20:43.560
the 3D points, we can apply bundle adjustment to refine the 3D reconstruction. More detail of

20:43.560 --> 20:48.480
bundle adjustment will be discussed. And the third paradigm which we will look at will be the

20:48.480 --> 20:53.720
hierarchical structure for motion. I will very briefly go through this technique in today's

20:53.760 --> 20:59.840
lecture. And the first thing that we will do here would be from the scene graph, we will do

20:59.840 --> 21:06.960
hierarchical clustering to get clusters of the scenes that are closely related to each other.

21:06.960 --> 21:13.240
So each one of these clusters could be seen as a strong connected component which are closely

21:13.240 --> 21:18.760
related to each other. We'll apply hierarchical clustering where the edges here will be weighted

21:18.760 --> 21:25.540
by the number of inliers between a pair of images that is computed from the robust tool

21:25.540 --> 21:31.920
view geometry that we have discussed earlier on. And once we get all these clusters in hierarchical

21:31.920 --> 21:37.120
structure for motion, the next thing that we will do is that we will do incremental structure for

21:37.120 --> 21:44.320
motion on each one of these cluster independently. So here is an illustration where this cluster we

21:44.320 --> 21:51.040
will do apply incremental structure for motion to get a certain view of this as well as to get

21:51.040 --> 21:58.840
the camera poses with respect to a world frame which we call FW1 over here. And then the next

21:58.840 --> 22:06.400
step we will do the similar approach on the next cluster over here. So all the 3D reconstruction

22:06.400 --> 22:12.520
as well as the camera poses would be with respect to another local world frame which we call FW over

22:12.520 --> 22:21.400
here and so on so forth. Then finally once we reconstructed all the 3D points and the camera

22:21.400 --> 22:27.840
poses of each respective cluster, the final step would be to merge all these clusters together to

22:27.840 --> 22:34.760
form a global 3D reconstruction. And this merging of the clusters together would be done using

22:34.760 --> 22:40.680
similarity transform. So this is similar to the absolute orientation algorithm that we have seen

22:40.680 --> 22:47.200
earlier on when we talk about the PNP problem. When we have all the 3D-3D correspondences which

22:47.200 --> 22:53.040
can be established from the 2D-2D correspondences between clusters, we can apply the absolute

22:53.040 --> 22:59.080
orientation problem to get the rotation translation. And finally we will just need one point

22:59.080 --> 23:07.400
correspondence to compute the scale difference between the two clusters before we can merge

23:07.400 --> 23:14.680
them all into a single consistent 3D model that looks something like this here.

